config NET_RTL819X
	bool "Realtek RTL8197F (RTL819x) switch-core Ethernet"
	depends on SOC_RTL8197F || COMPILE_TEST
	select PHYLIB
	select CRC32
	help
	  Ethernet MAC driver for the built-in switch-core CPU port of the
	  Realtek RTL8197F (RTL819x family) SoC.  This drives the CPU-port
	  descriptor DMA engine (swNic) that moves frames between the CPU and
	  the on-chip Ethernet switch.

	  bool (not tristate): the multi-object module mixes
	  module_platform_driver() (rtl819x-eth.c) with late_initcall()
	  (rtl865x_asichal.c) — two init_module definitions under =m — and the
	  /proc entries are never removed, so it cannot be built modular.

config RTL819X_HWNAT
	bool "Hardware NAT offload for the RTL8197F ASIC"
	depends on NET_RTL819X
	# Deliberately NOT "depends on NF_FLOW_TABLE": the flow table is a module
	# in OpenWrt and this driver is built in, but nothing here links against
	# it. The offload block helpers all live in net/core/flow_offload.c, which
	# is always built, and the flow table reaches us through ndo_setup_tc.
	help
	  Offload established IPv4 NAT flows into the SoC's ASIC NAPT table via
	  the netfilter flow table's hardware offload path
	  (ndo_setup_tc(TC_SETUP_FT)). Without this the datapath is correct but
	  every forwarded packet crosses the CPU.

	  Flows are offered as tc-flower rules on a flow block, the same path DSA
	  user ports forward to their conduit. Only the masquerade shape is taken
	  (IPv4 TCP/UDP, source NAT, no destination NAT); everything else is
	  declined and stays on the software fastpath, so this is an accelerator
	  and never a dependency.
